import "@/reqlan rq/phonebook.rq" as phonebook_reqs

stopgap {
  Stopgap distribution is at https://marketplace.visualstudio.com/manage/publishers/reqlan
}

version_management {
  Package versions are managed with Changesets via ["../../.changeset/config.json"].
  Publishable channels (each tag-driven):
  - Marketplace/Open VSX: ["./distribution.rq".extension_package] (private npm package; tagged `ext/v*`)
  - GitHub Pages: ["./distribution.rq".site_package] (tagged `site/v*`)
  - npm: `@reqlan/language`, `@reqlan/analytical`, `@reqlan/cli`, and `@reqlan/mcp` (tagged `language/v*`, `analytical/v*`, `cli/v*`, `mcp/v*`)
  Platform native packages (`@reqlan/analytical-<platform>-<arch>`) are not a separate tag channel; they ride `analytical/v*` and share `@reqlan/analytical`'s version ([rust_binary_distribution]).
  Contributors record release intent by adding a changeset file when a publishable package changes.
}

npm_distribution {
  npm publishing runs only when a `language/v*`, `analytical/v*`, `cli/v*`, or `mcp/v*` tag is pushed.
  Tags are created by ["./distribution.rq".deployment_flow]; the npm workflow does not publish on ordinary commits.
  The deploy workflow builds language/analytical/cli/mcp once, then publishes each package in sequence via `pnpm --filter <pkg> publish --access public --no-git-checks` (language, then analytical, then platform packages for [native_targets], then cli, then mcp).
  Before each package publishes, ["../../scripts/skip-already-published.mjs"] with `--filter <pkg>` checks the public npm registry and skips that step when the version is already published (sets `should_publish=false`). That avoids re-attempting already-published versions under OIDC.
  Auth uses npm trusted publishing (OIDC) from GitHub Actions — no long-lived `NPM_TOKEN`.
  Workflow requirements: `permissions.id-token: write`, Node ≥ 22.14 with npm ≥ 11.5.1, registry `https://registry.npmjs.org`.
  Each of `@reqlan/language`, `@reqlan/analytical`, `@reqlan/cli`, `@reqlan/mcp`, and every `@reqlan/analytical-<platform>-<arch>` platform package must have a Trusted Publisher on npmjs.com for GitHub Actions with org/user `littletuna4`, repository `reqlan`, workflow filename `deploy-npm.yml`, allowed action `npm publish`.
  Package `repository.url` must match `https://github.com/littletuna4/reqlan.git`. Provenance attestations are generated automatically for public packages from this public repo.
  Bootstrap: first publish of each scoped package can be done locally (you must be logged in to npm as an `@reqlan` org member) with `pnpm --filter @reqlan/language publish --access public --no-git-checks`, then analytical, then platform packages, then cli, then mcp — or wait for the trusted-publisher CI path after packages exist and Trusted Publisher is configured.
  Note: Packages must be `private: true` to skip local `changeset publish` (`pnpm run release:npm`), as with `reqlan-extension`. Platform packages are in a Changesets `fixed` group with `@reqlan/analytical` so they share its version; CI publishes them per-package after binaries are built and does not use `changeset publish` for those tarballs.
  Published packages declare `publishConfig.access: public`.
  Implementation: ["../../.github/workflows/deploy-npm.yml"], ["../../scripts/skip-already-published.mjs"], ["../../scripts/prepare-native-packages.mjs"], ["../../scripts/collect-napi-binary.mjs"], and root `release:npm` in ["../../package.json"] for local changeset-based publish. Docs: ["https://docs.npmjs.com/trusted-publishers"].
  @status in-progress
  @todo Trusted Publisher not yet configured on npmjs.com for each `@reqlan/analytical-<platform>-<arch>` package
}

native_targets {
  Canonical VS Code `targetPlatform` / npm `os`+`cpu` tuples for native distribution:
  - `win32-x64`
  - `win32-arm64`
  - `linux-x64`
  - `linux-arm64`
  - `darwin-x64`
  - `darwin-arm64`
  napi-rs package suffixes map as: `linux-*-gnu`, `darwin-*`, `win32-*-msvc` (gnu-only for linux; musl/alpine out of scope for the first matrix).
  Used by [rust_binary_distribution], [vsix_export], [extension_host_target], and ["./native_host_binary.rq".native_host_binary].
  Implementation: ["../../scripts/native-targets.mjs"].
  @status done
}

rust_binary_distribution {
  One platform-specific npm package per [native_targets] tuple: `@reqlan/analytical-<platform>-<arch>` (napi suffixes: `linux-x64-gnu`, `linux-arm64-gnu`, `darwin-x64`, `darwin-arm64`, `win32-x64-msvc`, `win32-arm64-msvc`).
  Each package declares `os` and `cpu`, contains the matching core engine `.node`, and is version-locked to `@reqlan/analytical` via the Changesets `fixed` group in ["../../.changeset/config.json"].
  `@reqlan/analytical` lists them as `optionalDependencies` so CLI/MCP install the host-matching binary at install time.
  Published tarballs pin those optionalDependencies to the same version (not `workspace:*`).
  Published on the existing `analytical/v*` channel ([npm_distribution] / [release_tagging]); not a separate tag channel.
  Website download listing and GitHub-release postinstall are later follow-ups (out of scope here).
  Engine port: ["../core_analysis/rust_port.rq".analytical_rust_port] / ["../core_analysis/rust_port.rq".native_bridge].
  Per-target VSIX packaging consumes the same platform packages ([vsix_export]).
  Implementation: ["../../packages/analytical-native/"], ["../../scripts/prepare-native-packages.mjs"], ["../../.changeset/config.json"], ["../../.github/workflows/deploy-npm.yml"] build-native job.
  @status in-progress
  @todo first analytical/v* release with platform packages not yet published to npm
  @todo Trusted Publisher registration for platform packages on npmjs.com
  @plan {
    goal
      Ship per-architecture core engine binaries beside `@reqlan/analytical` so CLI, MCP, and per-target VSIXes can load the Rust engine for the extension-host platform.

    constraints
      - optionalDependencies (not devDependencies) for runtime consumers.
      - Trusted publishing and `analytical/v*` tagging stay [npm_distribution] / [release_tagging].
      - gnu-only linux; musl and GitHub-release postinstall out of scope.
      - Website binary listing deferred.

    steps
      1. Scaffold six platform packages with os/cpu and the napi binary. (done)
      2. Wire optionalDependencies on `@reqlan/analytical`; version-lock via Changesets `fixed` group. (done)
      3. Cross-compile reqlan-napi in CI for [native_targets]. (done in deploy-npm.yml)
      4. Publish platform packages on `analytical/v*` after `@reqlan/analytical`.
      5. Prefer optionalDependency resolution in the loader; keep crates/target fallback for local dev by walking to repo-root `crates/Cargo.toml` (not `packages/crates` from `out/native`). (done)
  }
}

extension_host_target {
  The extension host must load the native core engine for its machine.
  The selection strategy is ["./native_host_binary.rq".native_host_binary].
  IndexService and WorkspaceIndex use `NativeWorkspaceIndex` (["../core_analysis/rust_port.rq".native_bridge]).
  If the engine is missing, activation fails.
  Loader: ["../../packages/analytical/src/native/load-native.ts"].
  The extension registers search folders from ["../../packages/extension/src/analytical_submodule/index.ts"].
  @status in-progress
  @todo first `@reqlan/analytical-<platform>-<arch>` release not yet published
  @tests (
    ["../../packages/analytical/test/load-native.test.ts"]
    ["../../packages/analytical/test/native-packaging.test.ts"]
  )
}

extension_package {
  The VS Code extension lives in packages/extension with npm package name `reqlan-extension` (Marketplace / Open VSX id `reqlan.reqlan-extension`).
  Its package.json version is the authoritative Marketplace and Open VSX release version.
}

site_package {
  The static site lives in site/ with npm package name `site`.
  Its package.json version identifies site releases for tagging.
}

deployment_flow {
  On push to main, if pending changesets remain, the release workflow applies them with `pnpm changeset version`.
  This bumps package.json for each affected package and writes changelogs.
  The workflow commits the result, creates annotated tags for every bumped publishable package, and pushes the commit and tags back to main.
  Release tags use `site/v{version}`, `ext/v{version}`, `language/v{version}`, `analytical/v{version}`, `cli/v{version}`, and `mcp/v{version}` where `{version}` is the semver from that package's package.json.
  Deployment is tag-driven for every channel per ["./distribution.rq".release_tagging]; the release workflow only versions and tags.
  GitHub Actions pushes with the default `GITHUB_TOKEN` do not trigger other workflows, so site and npm deploy are also invoked directly from the release workflow.
  Optional `RELEASE_PAT` allows tag pushes to trigger downstream workflows for manual reruns and external CI.
  Recursive runs are prevented by skipping the release workflow for release commits.
  Tag patterns and downstream deploy workflows follow ["./distribution.rq".release_tagging].
  The implementation lives in ["../../.github/workflows/release.yml"].
}

release_tagging {
  Deployment is tag-driven rather than commit-driven for every publishable channel.
  Tags are created automatically by ["./distribution.rq".deployment_flow], not manually.
  Site deployments are triggered by Git tags matching `site/v*` via ["./distribution.rq".site_distribution].
  VS Code extension publishing is triggered by Azure DevOps tags matching `ext/v*` via ["./distribution.rq".extension_distribution] and ["./distribution.rq".openvsx_registry].
  npm publishing is triggered by Git tags matching `language/v*`, `analytical/v*`, `cli/v*`, or `mcp/v*` via ["./distribution.rq".npm_distribution].
  Platform native packages ride `analytical/v*` (same version as `@reqlan/analytical`); they are not a separate tag prefix.
  The tag suffix after the channel prefix (`site/v`, `ext/v`, `language/v`, `analytical/v`, `cli/v`, `mcp/v`) equals the package.json version at that tag.
}

npm_package_readme {
  Publishable npm packages (`@reqlan/language`, `@reqlan/analytical`, `@reqlan/cli`, `@reqlan/mcp`) ship README.md generated from each package's `README.template.md`.
  Pattern matches [extension_readme]: centered logo, package name, description, features, phonebook links, and Changesets changelog body.
  Generated by ["../../scripts/generate-npm-package-readmes.ts"] via root `pnpm run build:readme`.
  Contact and repository links come from [phonebook_reqs.phonebook] via ["../../scripts/phonebook.ts"] per [phonebook_reqs.npm_package_readme_links].
}

extension_readme {
  The VSIX package must include a README for the Marketplace and Open VSX listings.
  Contact and repository links come from [phonebook_reqs.phonebook] via ["../../scripts/phonebook.ts"] per [phonebook_reqs.extension_readme_links].
  Release notes are appended from ["../../packages/extension/CHANGELOG.md"], which Changesets maintains per [version_management].
  Generated during extension build from ["../../packages/extension/README.template.md"] by ["../../packages/extension/scripts/generate-readme.ts"] per ["../development/build.rq".extension_readme_build].
}

root_readme {
  The GitHub repository README is the primary repo lander per ["./landers.rq".landers].
  Site, marketplace, repository, and contact links come from [phonebook_reqs.phonebook] via ["../../scripts/phonebook.ts"] per [phonebook_reqs.root_readme_links].
  Generated from ["../../README.template.md"] by ["../../scripts/generate-root-readme.ts"].
}

vsix_export {
  One VSIX is built per [native_targets] tuple via `vsce package --target <tuple> --no-dependencies`.
  Each VSIX keeps today's esbuild-bundled JS (TS dependency graph stays bundled); only the matching platform native package is staged into the VSIX so other targets' binaries are excluded.
  Stamp `targetPlatform` so Marketplace/Open VSX route by extension host ([extension_host_target], ["./native_host_binary.rq".native_host_binary_distributed]).
  Artifact name shape: `reqlan-extension-<version>-<target>.vsix`.
  The VSIX includes logo assets from the extension media build: PNG for marketplace icon, language file icon, activity bar, and README; WebP as a raster fallback.
  Implementation: ["../../scripts/package-extension-targets.mjs"], ["../../scripts/fetch-native-packages.mjs"], ["../../scripts/prepare-native-packages.mjs"], ["../../packages/extension/.vscodeignore"] (`native/`), ["../../azure-pipelines.yml"].
  @status in-progress
  @todo first ext/v* release with six target VSIXes not yet shipped
  @plan {
    goal
      Ship six host-targeted VSIXes, each containing only the core engine `.node` for that extension-host platform.

    constraints
      - Keep --no-dependencies for the bundled TS graph; stage only the matching native package.
      - Prefer npm platform packages when they exist (["../../scripts/fetch-native-packages.mjs"]). Azure VSIX packaging builds each [native_targets] tuple on its own agent and stages those artifacts; it must not fail when that `@reqlan/analytical` version is not on npm yet.
      - `vscode:prepublish` must not restage the host native addon (`--skip-native`); each VSIX `native/target.json` must equal that VSIX `--target`.

    steps
      1. Stage matching `@reqlan/analytical-<platform>-<arch>` into extension `native/` before vsce. (done)
      2. Package with `vsce package --target <tuple> --no-dependencies` for each [native_targets] entry; skip native restage in vscode:prepublish; verify packed `native/target.json`. (done)
      3. Publish each target to Marketplace and Open VSX ([extension_distribution], [openvsx_registry]).
  }
}

site_distribution {
  GitHub Pages deployment runs only when an `site/v*` tag is pushed.
  Tags are created by ["./distribution.rq".deployment_flow]; the site workflow does not deploy on ordinary commits.
  The static build embeds `/spec` via [site.spec_html_export], which needs the host `reqlan-napi` binary.
  The site workflow therefore builds and stages the host native engine (`scripts/stage-host-native.mjs` with `REQLAN_FORCE_NATIVE_BUILD=1`) before `pnpm run build`.
  The implementation lives in ["../../.github/workflows/deploy-site.yml"].
  @tests (
    ["../../packages/analytical/test/native-packaging.test.ts"]
  )
}

extension_distribution {
  Azure DevOps builds, tests, packages, and publishes the VS Code extension only for tags matching `ext/v*`.
  Tags are created by ["./distribution.rq".deployment_flow].
  The extension version comes from packages/extension/package.json at the tag; the pipeline must not overwrite it from the tag suffix.
  The implementation lives in ["../../azure-pipelines.yml"].
  The pipeline builds language, packages, and the extension only; it does not run the site static build or install Playwright ([site_distribution] owns site CI).
  Before tests it populates the host workspace optionalDependency (`scripts/ensure-host-native.mjs`) so `pnpm run test` loads the native engine the same way a production `@reqlan/analytical` install does.
  VSIX packaging follows ["./distribution.rq".vsix_export]: one VSIX per [native_targets] tuple; Marketplace selects the VSIX matching the extension-host target ([extension_host_target]).
  Marketplace publish is complemented by Open VSX per ["./distribution.rq".openvsx_registry].
  @status in-progress
  @todo first multi-target Marketplace publish not yet run
}

openvsx_registry {
  The extension should be published to the Open VSX registry at https://open-vsx.org so it is installable in editors that use Open VSX instead of the Visual Studio Marketplace.
  Publishing should use the same tag-driven `ext/v*` releases as ["./distribution.rq".extension_distribution].
  Each per-target VSIX from ["./distribution.rq".vsix_export] is published to Open VSX (Marketplace and Open VSX both select by extension-host `targetPlatform`).
  Azure DevOps is the authoritative publish workflow; Open VSX publish belongs in ["../../azure-pipelines.yml"] alongside marketplace deploy.
  @status in-progress
  @todo first multi-target Open VSX publish not yet run
}

workflow_deduplication {
  Extension publish automation should have a single authoritative workflow to avoid duplicate marketplace releases.
  Azure DevOps is the authoritative extension publishing workflow per ["./distribution.rq".extension_distribution] and ["./distribution.rq".openvsx_registry].
  The site remains deployed by GitHub Actions per ["./distribution.rq".site_distribution].
  npm packages are published by GitHub Actions per ["./distribution.rq".npm_distribution].
  Versioning and tag creation are owned by GitHub Actions per ["./distribution.rq".deployment_flow].
}


